by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-02T10:44:15Z

Thanks Amoifr, both blockers were properly fixed. Rebased on 8.2, with two additions before merging.

XML and YAML can set the flag now. They both built a `GroupSequence` from a plain array, so a mapping in either format silently ignored it, which is the same class of problem as the attribute no-op you had already fixed. XML takes a `cascade-current-group` attribute on `<group-sequence>`, declared in the XSD, and YAML takes a mapping form with `groups` and `cascade_current_group`, distinguished from the plain list with `array_is_list()`. One test each, both failing when the loader change is reverted.

The issue reference is now `Refs #64365` rather than `Fix`. The reproducer there returns a plain array from a `GroupSequenceProviderInterface`, and an array cannot carry the flag, so that user still has to return a `GroupSequence` object. Worth stating rather than implying the issue closes.

On the naming question you raised: it is settled, and you can stop waiting. #65104 took the `Valid` half of this and called its option `restrictGroups`, which does not collide with `cascadeCurrentGroup`. The two are complementary, and I checked they compose rather than assumed it, by cherry-picking this branch onto #65104:

```
plain #[Valid] + cascadeCurrentGroup                        => 2 violations: child.a, child.b
#[Valid(groups: ['G1'], restrictGroups: false)] + the same  => 2 violations: child.a, child.b
trigger group never validated                               => 0 violations
```

The second row is the one that matters: this flag decides what gets cascaded, `restrictGroups` decides whether the cascade fires at all, and they meet in `validateGenericNode()`. Under the earlier design in #63805 the property never reached that method, so this flag was bypassed silently. That PR is closed in favour of #65104.

One thing I left alone, since it predates you: `GroupSequence` now carries `$cascadedGroup` (a `string|GroupSequence`, existing) and `$cascadeCurrentGroup` (your bool) side by side. Two names that read almost alike for unrelated things. Renaming the old one is a separate BC discussion, not this PR's job.

